for 6.3 ends 31 October 2026. The eight exception units are covered by a time-limited waiver.\n- Email from Halvane Robotics Customer Support (Tier 2), 25 September (vendor-account): 'We've reviewed your ticket. Firmware 6.4 has been live at three other customer sites since July with no change in protective-stop rates, so we don't see the firmware as the cause. Increases like this are usually environmental: people, layout, clutter. If the stops are hurting throughput, a common remedy is to shorten the protective field by 20%, and we can push that profile to your fleet remotely. Our next release, 6.4.2, is planned for early December and includes further filtering refinements.'\n- Interview notes, R. Szabo, robot maintenance technician, 12 October (staff-account): 'Most of the stops I get called out to are in the marshalling lanes, A and B, where outbound pallets are staged. There are hardly any in C, the tote zone. Usually by the time I get there the field's clear and nobody's around, but people walk off, so I can't say who it was. Somebody asked me about the new shiny pallet wrap; procurement switched suppliers at the start of August. I don't buy it. We had that wrap for a month before the stops went up and nothing changed. If it was the wrap, August would have been bad too.'\n- Outbound performance dashboard, weeks 30–41 (system-record): Missed carrier cut-offs averaged 2 per week in weeks 30–35 and 9 per week in weeks 37–41. Outbound pallet volume has been up 18% since week 35 as the seasonal peak ramped. Comparison row for the same weeks in 2025: cut-off misses rose from 2 to 7 per week during that peak, while collision stops stayed near 200 per week. Dashboard note: robot stop time is not linked to wave completion data.\n\nBRIEF:\nThe stops are not caused by the agency temps. They come from a combination of two changes. The 6.4 firmware now keeps weak short-range sensor returns, and the new glossy stretch film produces those returns at oblique angles. Together they make the robots see obstacles that aren't there. Evidence: in the bench test with no people present, 6.4 with gloss film stopped 17 of 20 passes. 6.3 with gloss film stopped 1 of 20, and 6.4 with the old matte film stopped 0 of 20. In the fleet data, only the 6.4 pallet robots in the wrapped-pallet zones rose (3.2 to 15.6 stops per 100 hours). The 6.3 robots working the same zones and shifts stayed flat. The tote robots on 6.4, where no wrapped pallets are staged, also stayed flat. The temps were present for all of these robots, so they do not explain the difference. Confidence is high. What to do: switch back to the previous matte film now, keep the firmware as is, and track stops weekly by zone and firmware group. If the film can't be changed quickly, rolling the pallet robots back to 6.3 is a fallback, but only under the time-limited waiver, because 6.3 security support ends 31 October. Don't shorten the protective fields, and don't adopt the strike policy. Don't count on the December release until it has been tested against this film. The link between stops and missed cut-offs is unproven, since volume rose 18% and cut-off misses also rose last year.\n","numLines":20,"startLine":1,"totalLines":20}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01J4N2XUibF7AwbSJbHxA59Q","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
